coder v0.0.0-devel

USAGE:
  coder run [flags] <workspace> [-- command...]

  Run a command in a sandbox inside a workspace, with an identity of its own

  Run a command as a workload: in a sandbox that the workspace's daemon creates,
  without a workspace build. The workload gets its own identity, sponsored by
  you. It can do nothing in Coder unless you give it scopes, and it never
  receives your credentials. The command's output is streamed and its exit code
  returned.
    - Run a command and see who it is:
  
       $ coder run my-workspace -- coder identity
  
    - Let a workload list your workspaces:
  
       $ coder run my-workspace --scope workspace:read --scope template:read --
  coder list
  
    - Work in a sandbox with a terminal:
  
       $ coder run -it my-workspace -- sh
  
    - List the running workloads of a workspace:
  
       $ coder run --list my-workspace
  
    - List all of them, with what became of each:
  
       $ coder run --all my-workspace
  
    - Remove a workload:
  
       $ coder run --remove workload-1a2b3c4d my-workspace

OPTIONS:
      --agent string
          The workspace daemon to run the workload in, by name. Only needed when
          the workspace has several.

  -a, --all bool
          List the workspace's workloads, including the most recent ones that
          are over.

  -c, --column [name|status|sponsor|scopes|command|identity|sandbox|network|image|started|finished] (default: name,status,sponsor,scopes,command,started,identity)
          Columns to display in table output.

  -d, --detach bool
          Start the workload and print its name instead of following its output.

      --image string
          The image for the workload's sandbox. The workspace's template sets
          the default and decides which others are allowed.

  -i, --interactive bool
          Keep the command's input open. Use it with --tty, as -it.

  -l, --list bool
          List the workspace's running workloads instead of running one.

      --logs string
          Print the output of a workload, by name or identity, instead of
          running one. A running workload is followed until it ends. For one
          that is over, the end of its output was kept.

      --mount string-array
          A directory of the workspace to show in the sandbox, at the same path.
          It is read-only unless followed by ":rw". Repeat it for several. The
          workspace's template decides which directories may be shared; without
          this flag the workload sees nothing of the workspace.

      --name string
          A name for the workload.

      --network string
          What the sandbox can reach: "none" or "outbound". The workspace's
          template sets the default. With "none" the workload can still reach
          Coder, and nothing else.

  -o, --output table|json (default: table)
          Output format.

      --remove string
          Remove the named workload instead of running one.

      --scope string-array
          A permission the workload may use on your behalf, as an API token
          scope such as workspace:read. Repeat it for several. Without any, the
          workload can do nothing in Coder.

  -t, --tty bool
          Give the command a terminal and attach to it. Use it with
          --interactive, as -it.

———
Run `coder --help` for a list of global options.
